WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.260
So I'm going to turn this over to Sunghwan Kim

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.580
and he is going to take you through teaching PubChem

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.640
Hello everyone thank you very much for joining
us today.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:19.050
For the next 20 minutes or so I will talk
about how to use PubChem in the chemistry

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.710
classroom.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.279
So what is PubChem?

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:27.490
PubChem is a public chemical information resource
at NIH.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:33.300
Basically what it does is collect chemical
information from hundreds of data sources

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:39.570
in the public domain and disseminated back
to the public free of charge.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:44.440
PubChem is a very popular chemistry website.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:51.570
For example, last March we had more than 2.9
million unique users.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.200
Then who are our users?

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:03.829
Generally speaking, about a half of our users
are between age 18 and 24.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:10.470
And those in this age group are likely to
be college students.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:19.250
Many of them are required to take some chemistry
courses like general chemistry, organic chemistry.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.510
Then you may wonder how they use PubChem?

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:29.420
The most common use case of PubChem is a search
through Google.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:37.820
If you use Google to search for chemical by
name, usually it gives a PubChem record at

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.850
the top of the search result.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:46.000
Then click it to go to PubChem and get the
information.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:55.220
Basically, students are using PubChem as a
reference material like CRC handbook or Merck

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.440
index.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:04.610
Essentially, this has been a traditional activity
in the chemistry classroom for decades, or

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.900
perhaps centuries.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.230
Let's suppose that you want to find some property
of a chemical.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:16.930
Let's say the boiling point of ethanol.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:24.190
You can find it from PubChem much quicker
than from any hardcopy references . But at

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:30.880
the end of the day, what you learn from this
activity is still the boiling point of ethanol,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:34.000
regardless of where you find it.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:40.500
And the educational value of this activity
is not very different, regardless of what

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.340
reference you views.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:51.530
What additional educational values can PubChem
provide compared to the 19th century Merck

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:52.910
index?

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:59.530
To answer this question, we have to look at
PubChem in a different way.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:05.989
Most people view PubChem as a single information
resource.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:13.260
But you can also view PubChem as a gateway
to many information resources.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.639
In other words, PubChem provides access to
all sorts of chemical information scheduled

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:22.160
across hundreds of data sources.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:30.650
With that said, the students are now exposed
to an extremely large amount of data.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:39.430
So it is necessary for us to help them to
get the most out of this big chemical information.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:50.120
It means more emphasis of chemical education
should be given to chemical education.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:55.690
What can you teach using PubChem ? Here are
some examples.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:59.780
And we are going to review them briefly during
this webinar.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:08.440
First, PubChem can be used to help students
to build the ability to critically assess

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.850
chemical data.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.209
Today I will show you two examples.

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:19.209
The first one is about finding the boiling
point of benzene from PubChem.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:25.680
This is a screenshot for the PubChem compound
summary page for benzene.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:35.120
The compound summary page provides an aggregated
view of all information available in PubChem

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.480
for a given compound.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:44.349
If you scroll down this page, you will find
the boiling point section for benzene.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:52.100
Currently PubChem has six boiling point data
from five different sources.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:05:00.720
Some sources used Fahrenheit and others used
Celsius with various precisions.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:09.169
And once source explicitly specify the atmosphere
pressure at which the boiling point was measured.

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:12.870
Which is a good practice.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:21.129
And importantly, some gave a single value
as a boiling point while others gave a range

00:05:21.129 --> 00:05:22.419
of values.

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:29.439
Actually, one source gave two boiling points.

00:05:29.439 --> 00:05:35.080
One as a value and the other as a range of
values.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.009
What is going on?

00:05:37.009 --> 00:05:44.110
If you click these source names, the hidden
part it is a expanded and now you can see

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.759
one is for benzene and the other is for coal
tar.

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:56.910
And you can see that the links to the webpage
where the data was collected from.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:05.270
So what is happening here is that 176 is the
boiling point of benzene.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.050
But the other is for coal tar.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:14.540
Coal tar is a mixture of various hydrocarbons
including benzene.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:19.749
So you can get benzene from coal tar through
distillation.

00:06:19.749 --> 00:06:24.039
Basically, coal tar is the natural source
of benzene.

00:06:24.039 --> 00:06:29.949
So, some sources used benzene and coal tar
interchangeably.

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:36.060
And that is also reflected in PubChem through
data integration.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:45.090
So from this example, the students can learn
that there are multiple contexts of a chemical.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:49.379
And understand why they are integrated in
a single record.

00:06:49.379 --> 00:06:54.139
And realize that care should be taken when
searching for chemical data.

00:06:54.139 --> 00:07:03.490
The next example is about finding CAS numbers
for SIO two from PubChem.

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:08.740
In PubChem SIO two has 22 CAS numbers.

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:15.080
Each of these CAS numbers represent a different
context of SIO two.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:21.499
Depending on whether it means SIO 2 in general
or a specific form.

00:07:21.499 --> 00:07:27.689
Or solid SIO 2, can be amorphous or crystalline.

00:07:27.689 --> 00:07:33.880
The crystalline SIO 2 can be classified into
different polymorphs.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:43.699
And the property of SIO 2 depends on how it
is processed and whether it is pure or it

00:07:43.699 --> 00:07:46.669
contains some contaminants.

00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:54.050
In PubChem, all of these are aggregated into
a single record because they have the same

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.669
chemical formula.

00:07:55.669 --> 00:07:57.849
SIO 2.

00:07:57.849 --> 00:08:04.870
So again, from this example, students can
learn the importance of the context of chemical

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.290
data.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:13.290
Now the second one is about how to search
PubChem by chemical structure.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:19.440
You can type a chemical name into the Google
search box but what would you do if you don't

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.249
know the name of the compound but it's a structure?

00:08:24.249 --> 00:08:27.539
In PubChem, you have two options.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:35.530
First, you can draw the structure using molecular
drawing software called PubChem sketcher,

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:39.180
or use a line notation for the structure.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:45.820
Several line notations for chemical structures
have been developed but the most commonly

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:53.950
used ones are SMILES, InChI, and InChiKey
and all of these are supported in PubChem.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:09:00.480
Currently, PubChem has two structure search
interfaces.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:05.870
Chemical structure search is older but the
stable interface.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:15.680
And PubChem search beta uses newer technologies
but it was released as a test version.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:22.290
You can access the two structures search interfaces
from the PubChem homepage.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:29.139
And both interfaces support various types
of search including identity search, substructure,

00:09:29.139 --> 00:09:35.250
superstructure search, similarity search,
and molecular formulas search.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:44.370
And you can provide the infrastructure using
PubChem compound ID, or line notations like

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.500
smiles, InChI, and InChiKey.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:55.580
You can also draw the infrastructure using
the PubChem sketcher or upload a file that

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.580
contains the inpu structure.

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:07.940
And PubChem is actively working on a new interface
that will replace the two existing interfaces.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.630
So please stay tuned.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:17.460
Another important topic to cover is structure
clustering and structure activity analysis.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.579
Or SAR analysis.

00:10:19.579 --> 00:10:26.440
Structure clustering is about grouping compounds
into small clusters according to their structural

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:28.290
similarities.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:35.050
It is helpful in classifying molecules is
according to their structural characteristics.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:42.240
As an example, these are some anti-allergic
agents from PubChem.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.510
They can be grouped into three major clusters.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:51.890
Like dipeptides, glucocorticoids, and antihistamines.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:58.560
Based on these clustering results, you can
expect that the anti-allergic activities of

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:05.070
these compounds may come from three different
mechanisms of action.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:12.230
And SAR analysis is about clustering compounds
and bioassay data simultaneously.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:22.450
It is useful for identifying structural characteristics
responsible for the bio activity of molecules.

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:28.470
Unfortunately the structure clustering and
SAR analysis tools will be retired by the

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:37.290
end of this year but the same tasks can be
done using third-party software after downloading

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.130
necessary data from PubChem.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:48.029
The last one is automated data retrieval using
a computer code.

00:11:48.029 --> 00:11:53.010
PubChem provides multiple programmatic aspects
routes.

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:57.800
Among them PUG-REST is the simplest and easiest
to learn.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:07.019
It can be used with popular programming languages
like Python, so students can learn to automate

00:12:07.019 --> 00:12:13.740
data retrieval from PubChem by writing a short
script.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:18.509
Let me briefly talk about Cheminformatics
OLCC.

00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:26.240
It was an NSF funded project led by Professor
Belford at the University of Arkansas, Little

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:27.240
Rock.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:34.810
And PubChem was actively involved in this
project as part of our outreach effort for

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:38.760
the chemical education community.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:45.610
Teaching chemical cam informatics to undergraduate
chemistry majors has many unique challenges.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:53.480
First cheminformatics is not a field of chemistry
but a mix of many different fields.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:13:01.360
Because of that not so many faculty members
have the expertise to keep teach the course.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:09.750
In addition to that, there is no textbook
suitable for undergraduate chemistry majors.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.139
There are some cheminformatics textbooks.

00:13:12.139 --> 00:13:18.990
But most of them are written for computer
science majors or graduate students.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:29.470
Or cover narrow topics that focuses on medicinal
chemistry and drug discovery.

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:36.870
Cheminformatics OLCC aimed to address these
issues, at least partially.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:47.069
In Cheminformatics OLCC, experts were recruited
from various places including industry, government

00:13:47.069 --> 00:13:49.040
and academia.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:55.759
And they prepared reading materials and homework
problems sets.

00:13:55.759 --> 00:14:01.920
And they were made available online through
the OLCC website.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:09.329
And the instructors at multiple participating
schools used these online materials to deliver

00:14:09.329 --> 00:14:13.129
the Cheminformatics course at their schools.

00:14:13.129 --> 00:14:21.720
Concurrently the instructors had face to face
meetings with the students in the classroom

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:29.910
at the same time the Cheminformatics experts
got engaged in online discussions with students

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:33.170
through OLCC website.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:35.370
We ran this course twice.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:38.190
In fall, 2015.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:42.600
We had about 30 students from four schools.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:49.319
And in spring, 2017, there were about 50 students
from 10 schools.

00:14:49.319 --> 00:14:56.690
All the course materials developed during
this course are available at the OLCC website,

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:59.069
olcc.ccce.divched.org, and Libre Texts, chem.libretexts.org.

00:14:59.069 --> 00:15:07.790
So if anybody wants to use these materials
for his own course, he can use them for free.

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:13.319
And many of the course materials covered PubChem
data, tools, and services.

00:15:13.319 --> 00:15:21.770
Here, is a quick summary of PubChem related
topics covered in the Cheminformatics OLCC.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:27.529
Basically it includes everything I mentioned
today and more.

00:15:27.529 --> 00:15:34.350
In summary, a majority of PubChem users are
likely to be college students.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:37.430
And they use PubChem as a reference material.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:43.329
But, there are many other things that students
can do beyond this use case.

00:15:43.329 --> 00:15:50.740
PubChem can also be used as a free online
Cheminformatics education resource.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:54.910
And Cheminformatics OLCC is a good example.

00:15:54.910 --> 00:16:04.180
And finally, we are very interested in working
together with the chemical education community.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:10.480
So if you're interested, please reach out
to us for collaboration.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.779
Thank you and I am more than happy to answer
your questions.

00:16:13.779 --> 00:16:15.879
[Peter Cooper] Thank you Sunghwan.

00:16:15.879 --> 00:16:19.990
We will leave it open for a couple of minutes
to see if there are any questions.

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.550
We do have a couple of questions.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:24.840
Let me get to those.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:28.709
So there are two questions that relate to
the same thing.

00:16:28.709 --> 00:16:37.050
One, what third-party tools can be used to
replicate the SAR functions that will be discontinued?

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:43.910
[Sunghwan] We are not allowed to endorse any
commercial software or third-party software

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:47.889
so we cannot answer that question.

00:16:47.889 --> 00:16:53.529
But if you send me an email, I can tell you
about that.

00:16:53.529 --> 00:16:54.529
In person.

00:16:54.529 --> 00:16:58.240
[Peter] I think it is okay if you want to.

00:16:58.240 --> 00:17:00.060
To mention something.

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.060
We don't endorse it.

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:03.070
An example would be fine.

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:09.459
[Sunghwan] In my opinion, R is the best one,
it is free.

00:17:09.459 --> 00:17:13.960
It runs on PC and Mac and Linux.

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:18.900
And in the case of R there are many tutorials
out there.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.160
So you can start from there.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:23.760
[Peter] Thank you.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.840
I don't see any additional questions right
now.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:29.390
So thanks everybody for coming.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:33.670
And we are having another webinar next week
